****
Hydrogen Epsilon, in its full glory, is revealed when continuum subtraction is applied. 

The image presented here was obtained by subtracting a continuum, very much like it is done, 
traditionally, with the He I D3 and sometimes to the He I 10830 lines. 
The continuum was chosen from a few pixels away from the epsilon wavelength. 

Processing went further than continuum subtraction. The delta alone yields a grey-ish image, valueable for inspection, 
but far from the gist of a solar disk. It shows a grey background, a bright chromosphere and surface features. 
The delta was added-multiplied back onto the disk-at-wavelength, to produce this image. 

Filaments come alive, plages are dark-mottled, the chromospheric features are obvious, and flares light up.

This concept reveals that the signal is a perhaps more anemic version of the hydrogen delta image, 
which is a mottled-anemic version of beta, which in turn resembles the wing of hydrogen alpha.
